  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.92pt, "shows that the generated photovoltage is around -10 mV under normal incidence/flat mode, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.58pt, "similar results were also seen in the previous investigations [42], [43]. Changing the angle of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.34pt, "incident light in tilt modes 1 and 2 resulted in some minor change in the voltage, which is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 144.98pt, "around ±1 mV. Figure 7(b) shows the results of the device with PBTTT-C14 polymer layer on ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.74pt, "the ZnO micro rod matrix. The device, under light illumination, when tilted at different ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.38pt, "directions/angles, shows a large variation in the generated photovoltage. The developed device ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.14pt, "exhibits a change in photovoltage from positive to negative after crossing the normal incidence ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 227.78pt, "of illumination, which can be called the voltage flipping of the device under different ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.54pt, "illumination directions/angles. Therefore, this novel feature of the device enables it to detect ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.18pt, "the direction of the incident light. In Figure 7 (b), different tilt modes show different values ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 289.97pt, "and signs of the generated voltage. The complete process of photon absorption and its ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.61pt, "extraction into charges can be understood by following the steps: (i) When photons are ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.37pt, "absorbed within the organic semiconductor, bound excitons are generated, which then diffuse ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.01pt, "to the OI-HJ. The diffusion of the generated excitons to the interface produces charge transfer ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 372.77pt, "excitons. (ii) At this junction, the electron transfers to the inorganic layer via either a resonant ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 393.41pt, "or nonresonant process, depending on the relative energy gaps and offsets between the organic ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 414.17pt, "and inorganic materials [44]. The interaction forms a hybrid charge transfer exciton (HCTE), ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 434.81pt, "whose stability is mainly influenced by the permittivities of the materials in contact and the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 455.57pt, "effective mass of the electron in the inorganic material. (iii) The HCTE subsequently ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 476.23pt, "dissociates, and the resulting electron and hole are collected at the electrodes, returning the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 496.99pt, "organic semiconductor back to its ground state. The bandgap tuning in photo-active ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 517.63pt, "semiconductors plays a key role in electron excitation and charge transformation or extraction ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 538.39pt, "within or on electrodes. Hence, the efficiency and performance of the photo device can be ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 559.03pt, "directly affected by the bandgap tuning [45]. In the inorganic photodetector (without PBTTT-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 579.79pt, "14 in Figure 7 (a)), the photoactive semiconductor ZnO was decorated with Au NPs on its ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 600.43pt, "surface, which induced the surface plasmon resonance, enhancing the UV response of ZnO ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 621.19pt, "based photodetectors and generating -10 mV photovoltage under light illumination. This effect ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 641.83pt, "is due to the defect level emissions in ZnO micro rods, which cause surface plasmon resonance ")
